# Private C256 async B-ring draft

The experiment is saved as drafts only. Nothing has been compiled, installed,
timed or routed into the public model. The
[feasibility note](c256_cp_async_b_feasibility.md.draft) explains the hypothesis
and costs; [v29 hint results](cache_hint_v29_results.md.draft) explain why this
tests a different mechanism.

The private schema is:

```text
_inference_window_block_cp_async_b(
    state, w1, w2, w3, qkv_weight, projection_weight,
    ffn_scale, attn_scale, head_scale, bias,
    phase=0, packed_output=True, raw_output=False) -> (published, raw)
```

It accepts the existing dual canonical/vector prepared caches and FP16 or
packed-E4 input. Output is packed E4; optional raw output is FP16. C256,
inference-only and SM89+ restrictions match rolling. Unsupported output or
shape contracts raise; no public policy is added.

The source files to review and, later, activate are:

- [Kernel](../csrc/kernel_impl/window_block_cp_async_b.cuh.draft)
- [Launcher](../csrc/kernel_launcher/window_block_cp_async_b.cu.draft)
- [Torch API/schema](../csrc/torch_api/window_block_cp_async_b.cpp.draft)

The ring owns 24 KiB beside the unchanged 16-KiB A exchange. Two aligned
cache-all `cp.async` copies form each K group. The source schedule commits K0
and K1, issues K+2 before current work and waits 2/2/2/2/2/2/1/0 groups. Each
thread consumes its own two `uint4` values. No added CTA barrier or per-launch
carveout-setting call is needed. The last wait drains the ring before reuse
by the next dense call. The driver selects sufficient shared capacity; actual
carveout and residency remain unmeasured.

The [CPU proof report](../outputs/window_cp_async_b_cpu_proof.json.draft)
verifies the exact cache-byte inverse map, owner-disjoint slots, bounded
K0–K7 copies and unchanged ascending A/MMA order. Its source comparison
requires all code outside B operand delivery to match rolling, including
all seven CTA barriers. Sixteen CPU tests pass, including intentionally
missing waits, combined groups, early overwrite, changed addresses, bad
tails and arithmetic reordering. They do not establish compiled scheduling
or CUDA correctness.

Reproduce the CPU checks:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tools/window_cp_async_b_proof.py.draft
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/test_window_cp_async_b_schedule.py.draft
```

The [CUDA test draft](../tests/test_window_cp_async_b.py.draft) contains 62
cases after parameter expansion: shifted phases, short/tail fields, batch 2,
half/packed input, raw/no-raw output, all existing canonical/cache paths,
real checkpoint blocks 15/49, publication patterns, offset/strided storage,
nondefault-stream warmup, mutated graph replay, and sentinel-guarded immutable
operands. Invalid/empty contracts and backward rejection are included. These
tests have only been syntax-checked; they need activation and execution.

The [paired benchmark draft](../tests/benchmark_window_cp_async_b.py.draft)
defaults to rolling versus the ring across six phase-1 packed/no-raw contracts.
Each repeat shuffles warmed graph order; `--reverse` reverses that same order.
It retains every captured invocation's outputs, verifies that nonempty output
allocations do not overlap, and compares every output with an independent
eager composition after initial replay and after timing. These checks occur
outside the event interval. Seven CPU regression tests catch corrupt middle
outputs and aliased storage. A separate one-invocation graph performs the
poisoned replay check and is labeled with that narrower scope.

It preserves raw samples, exactness, input/cache immutability, replay evidence,
binary/device/source identities and a three-percent screen.
`--all-candidates` adds all seventeen other existing paths for nineteen total,
including both `canonical_public` with canonical weights and `cached_public`
with all five dual caches. Promotion compares against the minimum time of
all eighteen incumbents, excluding only `async_B_ring`. Without that
flag, the all-incumbent gate is explicitly unmeasured. Cache preparation is
outside resident timing and no native physical-I/O or 85% claim is made.

After activation, build, exactness tests and sanitizers, example measurements:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_cp_async_b.py --output outputs/window_cp_async_b_forward.json
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_cp_async_b.py --reverse --output outputs/window_cp_async_b_reverse.json
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_cp_async_b.py --all-candidates --output outputs/window_cp_async_b_all.json
```

Before timing, inspect retained async-copy issue PCs, group/wait order and
copy-to-first-shared-read distance, especially the loop tail. Require two
`LDS.128` B reads per step: scalarization changes the sixteen-byte-stride bank
pattern and could introduce conflicts. Check registers, spills, global-copy
count and actual shared allocation. Correct synchronization alone does not
guarantee early scheduling or speed. Keep the measured rolling path unchanged
unless this candidate passes the applicable correctness and performance gates.
